     "voice": "en-US-SteffanNeural",
     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "In nineteen fifty-two, a cartoon bakery needed a bigger oven, so it cut itself into one thousand paper pieces. You think buying stock is placing bets on a magical ticker screen, but it is just owning tiny physical slices of a real company. Corporations work with investment bankers to create and register shares before selling those pieces to the public. Morningstar UK reports that companies typically pay out dividend earnings twice or four times a year. That animated short was preserved by the Library of Congress because owning a stock just means collecting your share of the profits."
    }
